# Navigating State After a PHP Script Completes

When a POST request finishes executing your server-side logic, the HTTP connection is technically closed or waiting for a response before tearing down. If your script simply ends after running database queries or processing data, the browser cannot magically know that a new page needs to load. You need an explicit directive telling the client what to do next. The most common approach involves sending a redirect header.

In your `if($done)` block, you must issue a redirect before any HTML content is echoed. PHP handles this via the `header()` function. You construct a Location header pointing to the target URL and set the response status code to 302 (Found) or 303 (See Other).

```php
if ($done) {
    // The script must output headers before any body content
    header("Location: /return-to-dashboard.php", true, 302);
    exit(); 
}
```

The `true` parameter in the `header()` call is crucial. It forces PHP to send the status code along with the location. Without it, if you accidentally echo a whitespace character or a line break earlier in the script (common after including config files), the header will fail silently. Once that happens, you are stuck; the browser has already received an empty response or partial HTML and cannot be redirected mid-stream. Always pair `header()` with `exit()`. This halts execution immediately, preventing any subsequent code from running and accidentally outputting data that would corrupt the redirect response.

This mechanism relies on the client honoring the status code. The 302 code tells the browser to fetch the new URL using a GET request. This is vital for security and usability. If your POST script handles sensitive data, you don't want the user's input or session state sent back to the original form via an unintended mechanism. By forcing a GET request on the return page, you ensure the user lands on a clean state where they can view results or modify inputs safely.

Sometimes developers attempt to handle this logic entirely on the client side using JavaScript:

```javascript
if (done) {
    window.location.href = '/return-to-dashboard.php';
}
```

While valid, this approach has significant limitations. It fails if JavaScript is disabled in the user's browser. Furthermore, it introduces a race condition where the server might finish processing before the client even knows the action was successful. Relying on a server-side redirect ensures that the transition happens within the HTTP protocol itself, independent of the client's rendering capabilities.

If you are working within the Laravel ecosystem, the framework abstracts this away into a convenient helper. You can use `redirect()->route('dashboard')` or `redirect('/path')`. This method automatically handles the header injection and the exit, making it less error-prone than writing raw `header()` calls. Laravel also provides a `back()` helper to return to the referring URL, which is often useful for form submissions where you want the user to see their input again after validation succeeds.

The core principle remains consistent regardless of the framework: the server must explicitly instruct the client on what happens next. Failing to do so results in a page reload or an empty screen, leaving the user confused about whether the action completed successfully. By adhering to standard HTTP redirects, you maintain a predictable flow that works across all browsers and devices without relying on fragile assumptions about client-side behavior.
